A||||10||Alex4mula||Do all SBC V8 swaps require battery relocation?||||1||
Z||000000||Alex4mula||08-31-2001||01:37 PM||||I know TPIs and LT1s need more space in that area and that it will be good for weight distribution. But if I use a carburated V8, do I still need to relocate the battery? I'm just looking for items to save some $$ at the begining of the swap. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Hartz||08-31-2001||03:54 PM||||It would be awfully tight.  You can see from the picture below that there is some room, but not a lot.  You might be able to secure a smaller that stock battery in there, but that would probably cost more than just a cheap battery relocation kit.  Search the forum - there are low-cost alternatives to relocate the battery.  I'd bet you could easily do it for under $50.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/engine_3.JPG"> <P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pontiaddict||08-31-2001||07:50 PM||||You could move the battery to the other side of the engine bay, where the stock v6 air cleaner used to be. You should be able to do that cheaply.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex4mula||09-02-2001||08:22 AM||||Thanks guys. I see (& have seen) the problem with TPI. I guess is the head that gets very close to that area. Anyone has a close look picture for a carbed V8? TIA.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bazooka||09-02-2001||10:45 AM||||    <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Alex4mula:<BR><B>I know TPIs and LT1s need more space in that area and that it will be good for weight distribution. But if I use a carburated V8, do I still need to relocate the battery? I'm just looking for items to save some $$ at the begining of the swap. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>********************************************<BR>You could locate the battery in the trunk on the passenger side just in front of the tail lite. This way it would take away very little space and would make access to the battery ideal.<BR> The relocation of the battery to the front (under the spare) is not as comfortable as it sounds. You would have to remove a very tight fitting spare tire that sits over the rear edge of the battery. That means removing the Hold-down for the tire, the jack and then the spare (which is a *****  to pull out). Also the steering dampener has to be removed altogether for the battery box in the front. The 2 or 4 gauge cables you need are much longer if they have to be routed all the way to the front (not cheap). <BR>   Trunk mounted batteries are fairly economical (see Rich Warbers Purple Pavement Eater that is For Sale). The 'Optima' style battery is also an option since it is smaller in physical size and can be mounted in almost any position. <BR>***************************************** <BR> <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html</A> <P>*****************************************<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Bazooka (edited 09-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||09-02-2001||02:09 PM||||I used CM West's "We Everywhere" battery box and the steering stabilizer stays and the spare tire and all mounting stays.<P>To get the stablizer off, you have to loosed the rack hold-down clamps a bit to get one bolt off.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||09-02-2001||02:58 PM||||Here is a few pics of carb'd V-8's.<P>The first one has an electronic water pump where the battery normally goes.<BR>The second one is open so they routed the coolant hose thru there.<BR>As you can see the engine goes up to the decklid hinge. Aside from the coolant hose you might be able to leave the battery there.<P><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Drag1.jpg">  <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/4k001.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 09-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Haze_Performance||09-03-2001||02:04 AM||||   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Drag1.jpg">   <P><BR>DROOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOLLLLLLL  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif"> <BR><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/indyclick.gif">             <BR></A><BR><A HREF="http://www.clubs.yahoo.com/clubs/ws6fiero" TARGET=_blank>WS6 Fiero Club</A>||||reg||11
Z||000008||MrPAW||09-04-2001||03:15 PM||||When I did my conversion I relocated the battery to the drivers side of the engine compartment up against the trunk wall.<BR>  <BR>I used two heavy duty "L" shaped shelf brackets from the local hardware store and a heavy duty universal battery tray with hold down. <P>The brackets are bolted upside down through the wall of the trunk and the battery tray is bolted on top.<P>Advantages to this method: Battery is easy to access,  Cables are short - max cranking power, Large battery will fit, No trunk space used, Still have spare tire, VERY inexpensive ~ $30 with new cables.<P>Disadvantages : Weight is still very close to back of car, Exaust manifold is only about 1" from end of battery - battery case gets pretty hot on summer days. <P>I am going to build an insulated shield to try and remedy the heat situation.||||reg||1
Z||000009||artherd||09-04-2001||03:48 PM||||personally I would not attempt a V8 swap if simply moving the battery were a 'too expensive, too much trouble' issue....<P>I think moving the battery upfront below the CG line of the car helps ANY fiero, let alone V8, and can be done with less than $150 in off-the-shelf parts, far less if you have fabrication skills of your own.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000010||Alex4mula||09-04-2001||04:53 PM||||Thanks all for the tips and photos. I guess I'll end up putting it up front but with some kind of accesible switch to easily disconnect it when necessary (i.e. not taking out the spare etc.). ||||reg||1
